        <div id="wh-and-w" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Watt-Hours Are the Tank, Watts Are the Pipe
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every power station has two headline numbers, and confusing them is the most expensive mistake buyers make. <strong>Watt-hours (Wh)</strong> measure stored energy, the size of the tank. <strong>Watts (W)</strong> measure how fast the inverter can deliver that energy, the width of the pipe. A unit with a big inverter and a small battery can start your refrigerator but will drain in a few hours.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check output first. If a load's running draw, or its starting surge, exceeds the station's continuous or surge rating, the inverter shuts off. A refrigerator might run at 100 to 200 watts but need 1,000 watts or more for a split second at compressor start. That is why a 300-watt station cannot run a fridge no matter how full it is, while a 1,500-watt unit can.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then check capacity. Divide the station's watt-hours by your load's average draw in watts to get rough hours, then knock off 10 to 15 percent for inverter losses. A 1,024Wh station feeding a 100-watt average load gives about 9 hours on paper. The word average matters: a refrigerator cycles on and off, so its average is far below its running draw, which is why a 1kWh station can carry one for most of a day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Surge modes deserve skepticism. Features with names like X-Boost or Power Lifting run resistive loads such as kettles and hair dryers by lowering the voltage, so the appliance runs slower or cooler. They do not let a station start a motor that exceeds its true surge rating, and they do nothing for capacity. Treat the continuous watt rating as the real one.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE QUICK FILTER</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">For a refrigerator, start at 1,000Wh and 1,500W.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Below that, a station is for phones, lights, routers, fans and CPAPs. Above it, you are buying more hours, not more capability, until you reach the 2kWh class that can carry a fridge and freezer through a full day.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="chemistry" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. LiFePO4 vs NMC: Why the Battery Chemistry Matters
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most current stations use <strong>lithium iron phosphate</strong> (LiFePO4 or LFP) cells. Older and some premium units use <strong>lithium nickel manganese cobalt</strong> (NMC). The difference shows up in three places: lifespan, weight and thermal behavior.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Trait</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">LiFePO4 (LFP)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">NMC</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Rated cycle life</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Typically 3,000 to 4,000+ cycles to 70-80%</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Typically 500 to 1,000 cycles to 80%</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Weight for same capacity</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Heavier</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lighter</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Thermal stability</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Very stable; resists thermal runaway</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Less tolerant of abuse and heat</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cold-weather charging</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Should not be charged below freezing unless heated</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Also limited below freezing</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Best fit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Home backup, frequent cycling</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lightweight camping, occasional use</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            For home backup, LFP is the better choice almost every time. A rated life of 3,000 cycles means you could fully cycle the station every day for eight years before it falls to about 80 percent capacity. Even heavy users will retire the unit for other reasons first. NMC stations are fine for occasional camping, but if you plan to use one as a daily solar buffer, the cycle count will catch up with you.
          </p>

          <p>
            The thermal argument matters indoors. LFP cells are much less prone to thermal runaway, the chain reaction behind lithium battery fires. That is not a license to bury a station under blankets or leave it in a hot car, but it is a real margin of safety for a device that lives in your house. Look for UL listings or equivalent certifications on the station itself.
          </p>

          <p>
            All lithium batteries age with time as well as cycles, and both chemistries dislike being stored at 100 percent charge in a hot garage. That is why the storage advice later on this page matters more than which badge is on the box.
          </p>

          <p>
            One practical note on cycle ratings: manufacturers count a cycle as one full discharge equivalent, so using half the battery twice counts as one cycle. Most outage owners use a station a few dozen times a year, which means an LFP unit will likely outlast its electronics, its warranty and probably the ports standard it ships with. For that use, chemistry is really a safety and weight decision, and LFP wins the safety side.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="runtime" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Runtime Math for Common Outage Loads
          </h2>

          <p>
            Use the table below as a starting point, then check your own appliance labels or plug them into an inexpensive watt meter for a day. Runtimes assume roughly 85 percent usable energy after inverter losses, which is typical for this class.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
