                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">40-100W average</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Will not start</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">8-21 hrs</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">17-42 hrs</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">LED lights, 4 bulbs</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">40W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5 hrs</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">21 hrs</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">42 hrs</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Laptop charging</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">45-90W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-4 charges</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">9-18 charges</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">18-36 charges</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Refrigerators deserve a closer look because they are the reason most people buy. A modern full-size fridge uses about 1 to 2 kWh a day, depending on size, age, ambient temperature and how often the door opens. Keep the door closed, set the temperature to a safe 37 to 40 degrees, and the average draw drops. An older fridge or a second fridge in a hot garage can use far more.
          </p>

          <p>
            Heating and cooling are where stations hit a wall. A 1,500-watt space heater would drain a 1kWh station in well under an hour, and a window air conditioner in a couple of hours. Electric resistance cooking is similar. If those loads matter in your outages, you need a generator, solar on a large station, or both. A gas furnace blower is more realistic: a few hundred watts while running, which a 2kWh station can carry for a number of hours of cycling.
          </p>

          <p>
            For CPAP users, the humidifier is the variable. Running with heated humidification can double or triple draw. Many manufacturers sell DC cables that bypass the station's inverter and stretch runtime noticeably. Ask your equipment supplier what your specific machine draws at your pressure setting.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="charging" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Charging: Wall, Solar, Car and Generator
          </h2>

          <p>
            A station is only as useful in a long outage as your ability to refill it. Wall charging is the fastest; current 1kWh LFP units commonly reach 80 percent in under an hour. That matters during rolling blackouts and when you run a generator in short bursts.
          </p>

          <p>
            Solar is what turns a station into multi-day backup. Real-world output from a portable panel is usually well below its label rating, often 50 to 75 percent in good sun, less with clouds, heat or poor angle. A 200-watt panel might put 600 to 1,000 watt-hours into a station on a good summer day and much less in winter. Match the panel's voltage and wattage to the station's solar input limits, and orient it toward the sun several times a day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Car charging through a 12V socket is slow, often around 100 watts, and idling a car for hours is wasteful. It is useful for topping up on the move, not for refilling a large battery. Never idle a vehicle in an attached garage to charge anything; the carbon monoxide risk is the same as with a generator.
          </p>

          <p>
            Generator charging is the most practical outage hybrid. A small inverter generator running outdoors, at least 20 feet from the house with exhaust aimed away, can refill a 1kWh station in an hour or two, after which the generator goes off and the station runs silently overnight. That pairing uses far less fuel than running a generator all night. See our <a href="/best-small-generator-for-home/">small home generator picks</a> for quiet inverters that suit this role.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical Refill Times, 1kWh LFP Station</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
