          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="outage-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Using a Power Station Well During an Outage
          </h2>

          <p>
            Plug loads in by priority. First the things that keep people safe: medical devices, a phone for emergency calls, a light for every room you will move through at night. Second, food: the refrigerator and freezer. Third, information: the router, modem and a TV or radio. Comfort loads come last, if at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            Run the refrigerator on a schedule if capacity is tight. A closed fridge holds safe temperatures for around four hours and a full freezer for much longer. Powering the fridge for an hour or two at a time several times a day can stretch a 1kWh station across a second day while keeping food safe. A cheap fridge thermometer removes the guesswork.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not plug a power station into a wall outlet to feed your house circuits; that is the same backfeed hazard as with a generator. Some large stations work with dedicated transfer switches or home panels made by the same manufacturer, installed by an electrician. For everything else, run extension cords directly to the appliance.
          </p>

          <p>
            Turn off the AC inverter when you are only charging phones through USB ports. Inverters draw power themselves, often 10 to 30 watts at idle, which can waste a meaningful slice of a small battery overnight. Most stations also have a standby timeout you can set in the app.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep a simple log during a long outage: the battery percentage each morning and evening, and what was plugged in. After a day you will know your real consumption far better than any table, and you can decide whether to cut a load, run the fridge in shorter blocks or fire up the generator outdoors for a recharge. Owners who track it commonly find the router, a forgotten phone charger and the inverter idle draw add up to more than they expected.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="features" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Features That Matter and Features That Do Not
          </h2>

          <p>
            Once capacity, output and chemistry are settled, the remaining features separate a station you will enjoy owning from one that annoys you. Start with the outlet panel. Count how many standard three-prong AC outlets you need at once, and make sure the important loads are not fighting for space with bulky plugs. Two or three USB-C ports rated at 60 to 100 watts or more let you charge laptops without running the inverter at all, which saves energy.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>UPS or EPS mode</strong> is worth having if you will leave the station plugged in to protect a modem, router, desktop or aquarium pump. The station passes wall power through and switches to battery when the grid drops. Switchover times of around 10 to 30 milliseconds are typical for this class, which keeps most electronics running. Treat it as a convenience feature, not a medical-grade backup; for life-sustaining equipment, follow the device maker's guidance on backup power.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Expansion ports</strong> let you add a second battery later instead of buying a second station. That is useful if you are not sure how much capacity you need, but expansion batteries usually cost nearly as much per watt-hour as a whole new unit, and they are tied to one brand and model generation. If you already know you want 2kWh, buying a 2kWh station outright is often cheaper than a 1kWh station plus an add-on.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Apps</strong> range from genuinely useful to filler. The useful parts are charge-speed control, which lets you charge gently for battery health or fast before a storm, charge limits for storage, and remote monitoring of input and output. Be wary of a station whose key settings are only reachable through an app; a clear front display and physical buttons still matter when phones are low or the internet is down.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Warranty and support</strong> are underrated. Major brands now offer around five years on their LFP stations, and that tells you something about expected battery life. Check whether the warranty requires registration, whether batteries are covered for capacity loss, and whether the company has a domestic service process. A cheap no-name station with a one-year warranty is rarely the bargain it appears to be.
          </p>

          <p>
            Features that matter less than marketing suggests: wireless charging pads on top, built-in speakers, and very large peak surge numbers quoted without a continuous rating beside them. Noise is worth checking, though; fan noise during fast charging is the most common complaint in owner feedback, and it matters if the station lives in a bedroom.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-generator" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Power Station or Gas Generator?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The two are complements more than competitors. A station is silent, has no exhaust, can sit next to your bed, needs almost no maintenance and starts instantly. A generator makes energy for as long as you have fuel, and fuel is cheap compared with batteries. Per kilowatt-hour delivered over a long outage, gasoline wins by a wide margin; per hour of quiet, safe indoor power, the battery wins.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Factor</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Power station</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Gas generator</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Indoor use</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Never</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Noise</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Silent to fan hum</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Loud open-frame; moderate inverter</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Multi-day outage</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Needs solar or a generator</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, with fuel supply</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Heavy loads (AC, well pump)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Only the largest units</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Maintenance</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Charge every few months</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fuel, oil, monthly runs</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cost per kWh of capacity</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low after purchase</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Choose a station alone if your outages are short, you live in an apartment or condo, or you mainly need to protect a CPAP, a fridge and internet. Choose a generator if outages last days, you need heat, a well pump or air conditioning, or you have a sump pump that must keep running. Choose both if you want silent nights and long-outage resilience; it is the most comfortable setup on this site.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">home generator rankings</a> cover the fuel-powered side, including transfer switches and safe placement, and our <a href="/best-gas-generator/">gas generator guide</a> covers fuel storage and run times.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Safety, Storage and Battery Lifespan
          </h2>

          <p>
            Power stations are among the safest backup options, but they are still large lithium batteries. Keep them on a hard surface with the vents clear, out of direct sun, away from water and away from anything that could fall on them. Do not charge or run them while covered. Use the charger and cables the manufacturer supplies or specifies.
          </p>

          <p>
            Store a station partially charged. Most manufacturers recommend somewhere between about 50 and 80 percent for long storage, at moderate indoor temperatures, and topping up every three months or so. Being stored full in a hot garage is the fastest way to age the cells. Before storm season, charge it fully and leave it plugged in only if the manufacturer supports continuous charging or UPS mode.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cold matters for charging. Lithium cells, LFP included, should not be charged below freezing unless the station has built-in battery heating; many units simply refuse to charge in the cold to protect themselves. If you keep a station in an unheated shed or vehicle, bring it inside to warm up before you charge it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch for damage. A swollen case, unusual smell, hissing or heat when idle are reasons to stop using it, move it outdoors away from structures if you can do so safely, and contact the manufacturer. Recycle old units through a battery recycler rather than the trash; most manufacturers and many home centers take them back.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="size-guide" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Which Size Should You Buy?
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Under 300Wh</strong> (RIVER 3, EB3A): phones, laptops, LED lights, fans, the router and a CPAP for a night or two. The right first purchase for apartments, dorms and anyone whose outages are a few hours long. Under $300 for a good LFP unit.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>About 1,000Wh</strong> (DELTA 2, Explorer 1000 v2, SOLIX C1000): a refrigerator for most of a day, plus internet and lights. The best value class for home backup and the one most households should buy. Pick the DELTA 2 or C1000 if you want expansion later, the Jackery if you want lighter and simpler.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>1,500 to 2,000Wh and up</strong> (Yeti 1500X, AC200L): a fridge and freezer through a full day, a CPAP for a week, or several days of essentials when paired with solar. These are heavy appliances; buy one when you know you will use the capacity. Beyond about 3kWh, you are in home-battery territory, and our <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">outage generator guide</a> covers larger units like the EcoFlow Delta Pro.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever you buy, add a watt meter and a fridge thermometer. Knowing your real loads is what makes a station last as long as you need it to.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on watt-hours alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A big battery behind a small inverter cannot start a refrigerator or pump. Check that continuous output covers your largest load's running draw and that the surge rating covers its start-up. Then check capacity for hours. Both numbers have to fit, and the continuous rating, not a boost mode, is the one to trust.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting it to run a space heater</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Resistance heaters, electric kettles and hair dryers draw 1,200 to 1,500 watts. A 1kWh station empties in well under an hour feeding one. If you need heat in outages, a gas furnace blower is a realistic load; a space heater is not, unless you have a generator or a very large battery bank.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the solar panel's label</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 200-watt portable panel rarely delivers 200 watts. Angle, heat, haze and season commonly cut real output to half or less of the rating. Plan solar recharge around a conservative daily figure, buy more panel than the math suggests, and reposition panels several times a day to follow the sun.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving it full in a hot garage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Lithium batteries age fastest when stored at full charge and high temperature. A station left at 100 percent in a summer garage loses capacity it never gets back. Store it indoors at about half to 80 percent charge, top up every few months, and charge fully only before storm season.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Idling a car in the garage to charge it</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Some owners try to recharge a station from a running vehicle in an attached garage. That produces the same deadly carbon monoxide as a generator indoors. Charge from the car only while driving or with the vehicle fully outdoors and away from openings, or use wall, solar or an outdoor generator instead.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Roughly how long does a 1,000Wh station keep a fridge cold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most modern full-size refrigerators average 60 to 150 watts over a day because the compressor cycles. After inverter losses, a 1,000Wh station typically runs one for roughly 6 to 14 hours. Older, larger or garage fridges use more. Keep the door closed and consider powering the fridge in blocks of an hour or two to stretch the battery across a longer outage.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a portable power station indoors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Power stations have no engine and produce no exhaust, so there is no carbon monoxide risk, which is their main advantage over generators. Keep them on a hard surface with vents clear, away from heat, water and flammable materials, and do not cover them while charging or running. Any fuel-powered generator used to recharge one must stay outdoors.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a power station run a CPAP all night?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually, yes. Most CPAPs draw 30 to 60 watts without the heated humidifier, so even a 250Wh station can cover a night, and a 1,000Wh unit can cover several. Heated humidification and high pressure settings raise draw substantially. A manufacturer DC cable avoids inverter losses. Confirm your machine's actual draw with your equipment supplier.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does LiFePO4 justify a higher price?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For home backup, yes. LiFePO4 stations are typically rated for 3,000 or more cycles before dropping to 70 to 80 percent capacity, several times the life of older NMC batteries, and the chemistry is more thermally stable. The extra weight matters for backpacking but not for a unit that lives in your house. The price gap has largely closed anyway.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a power station power my whole house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not a portable one. Even large expandable systems in the 3 to 6 kWh range cover essentials for a day or two, not central air, electric heat or an electric range. Whole-house battery backup means a permanently installed home battery system with professional installation. For whole-house coverage at lower cost, a standby or large portable generator is still the usual answer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many solar panels do I need to recharge a power station?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Plan on real output of about half the panel rating in decent sun. A 1,000Wh station refilled in one day needs roughly 200 to 400 watts of panels in summer and more in winter. Stay within the station's solar input voltage and wattage limits, which are listed on its spec sheet, and wire panels accordingly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the right way to keep a station between outages?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Store it indoors at moderate temperature with the battery around 50 to 80 percent, and check and top it up every three months or so. Avoid hot garages and car trunks. Before storm season or a forecast outage, charge it to full. Some models support staying plugged in as a UPS; follow the manufacturer's guidance on continuous charging.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

